"Every evening, the weather report tells you about a day that has not happened yet. It says how hot tomorrow will be, and whether you will need an umbrella. How can anyone know the future? Nobody can know it for certain. But the air follows rules, and that makes a good guess possible. A forecast begins with measuring. All day and all night, weather stations on the ground measure the temperature, the air pressure, the wind, and the rain. Balloons carry small instruments high into the sky, and hundreds of them are sent up around the world every day. Radar sees where rain is falling, and satellites look down at the clouds from space. Together they give a picture of the air right now. Next comes the computing. The air follows the rules of nature: warm air rises, wind blows from high pressure to low pressure, and water vapor turns into cloud. Scientists have written these rules as mathematics. A very powerful computer starts with the picture of the air right now and works out what it will be like a few minutes later, and then a few minutes after that, again and again, until it reaches tomorrow and the days after. But there is a problem. Nobody can measure the air perfectly. There are not enough instruments, and each one is a little bit wrong. These small mistakes do not stay small. They grow as the computer works its way into the future. So a forecast for tomorrow is usually good, and a forecast for next week is much less sure. Scientists think that about two weeks is the limit. Forecasters have a clever way to deal with this. They run the computer many times, and each time they change the starting picture just a little. If almost every run brings rain, rain is very likely. If only a few runs bring rain, it is less likely. In the end, the forecaster studies all the information and gives the chance of rain as a number. Now you can read that number. In Taiwan, a 70% chance of rain for Taipei means there are seven chances in ten that rain will fall in Taipei during that period. It does not mean that it will rain for 70% of the day or on 70% of the city. And if it stays dry on a 70% day, the forecast was not wrong. Three times in ten, that is exactly what should happen.": "7b02efd8b32b",
